1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,460
 Tanya Cushman Reviewer. Tanya Cushman

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:09,760
 Hi, everyone.

3
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:15,240
 Two years ago, my life changed forever.

4
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:17,800
 My wife, Kelsey, and I

5
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:22,100
 welcomed our daughter, Leela, into the world.

6
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:26,980
 Now, becoming a parent is an amazing, amazing experience.

7
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:30,180
 Your whole world changes overnight,

8
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,020
 and all of your priorities change immediately,

9
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:38,040
 so fast, that it makes it really difficult to process sometimes.

10
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,600
 Now, you also have to learn a tremendous amount about being a parent.

11
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:46,200
 Like, for example, how to dress your child.

12
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:48,980
 This was new to me.

13
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:52,240
 This is an actual outfit. I thought this was a good idea.

14
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:56,740
 And even Leela knows that it's not a good idea.

15
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,440
 So, there was so much to learn, and so much craziness all at once.

16
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:08,180
 And to add to the craziness, Kelsey and I both work from home.

17
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:10,360
 We're entrepreneurs. We run our own businesses.

18
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:16,600
 So, Kelsey develops courses online for yoga teachers.

19
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:17,640
 I'm an author.

20
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:20,480
 And so, I'm working from home. Kelsey's working from home.

21
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:21,780
 We have an infant.

22
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:25,840
 And we're trying to make sure that everything gets done that needs done.

23
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:31,120
 And life is really, really, really busy.

24
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:36,060
 And a couple weeks into this amazing experience,

25
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:41,280
 when the sleep deprivation really kicked in, like around week eight,

26
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:49,420
 I had this thought, and it was the same thought that parents across the ages, internationally,

27
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,920
 that everybody has had this thought, which is,

28
00:01:52,260 --> 00:01:57,480
 I am never going to have free time ever again.

29
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:01,220
 And somebody said it's true.

30
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:09,480
 Yeah, it's not exactly true, but it feels really, really true in that moment.

31
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:18,220
 And this was really disconcerting to me, because one of the things that I enjoy, more than anything else, is learning new things.

32
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:24,600
 Getting curious about something, and diving in, and fiddling around, and learning through trial and error,

33
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:27,080
 and eventually becoming pretty good at something.

34
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:35,760
 And without this free time, I didn't know how I was ever going to do that ever again.

35
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:41,420
 And so, I'm a big geek, I want to keep learning things, I want to keep growing.

36
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:47,380
 And so, what I decided to do was go to the library, and go to the bookstore,

37
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:51,840
 and look at what research says about how we learn, and how we learn quickly.

38
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:58,720
 And I read a bunch of books, I read a bunch of websites, and trying to answer this question,

39
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:01,720
 how long does it take to acquire a new skill?

40
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,420
 You know what I found?

41
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:07,680
 10,000 hours.

42
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:10,260
 Anybody ever heard this?

43
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:14,980
 It takes 10,000 hours, if you want to learn something new, if you want to be good at it,

44
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:16,820
 it's going to take 10,000 hours to get there.

45
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:20,900
 And I read this in book after book, and website after website,

46
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:28,000
 and my mental experience of reading all of this stuff was like,

47
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:28,780
 No!

48
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,000
 I don't have time.

49
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,040
 I don't have 10,000 hours.

50
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:39,660
 I am never going to be able to learn anything new ever again.

51
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,220
 But that's not true.

52
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:46,160
 So, 10,000 hours, just to give you a rough order of magnitude,

53
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:50,920
 10,000 hours is a full-time job for five years.

54
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:53,320
 That's a long time.

55
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,140
 And we've all had the experience of learning something new,

56
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:59,320
 and it didn't take us anywhere close to that amount of time, right?

57
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:00,780
 So, what's up?

58
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,240
 There's something kind of funky going on here.

59
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,820
 What the research says and what we expect and have experience is,

60
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:08,540
 they don't match up.

61
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:11,860
 And what I found, here's the wrinkle.

62
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:18,600
 The 10,000-hour rule came out of studies of expert-level performance.

63
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:21,960
 There was a professor at Florida State University,

64
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:23,740
 his name is K. Anders Ericsson.

65
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:25,960
 He's the originator of the 10,000-hour rule.

66
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:30,380
 And where that came from is he studied professional athletes,

67
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:35,240
 world-class musicians, chess grandmasters,

68
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:39,980
 all of these ultra-competitive folks in ultra-high-performing fields,

69
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:42,140
 and he tried to figure out,

70
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:46,320
 how long does it take to get to the top of those kinds of fields?

71
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:49,200
 And what he found is, the more deliberate practice,

72
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:53,500
 the more time that those individuals spent practicing the elements

73
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:54,680
 of whatever it is that they do,

74
00:04:55,140 --> 00:04:56,860
 the more time you spend, the better you get.

75
00:04:56,860 --> 00:05:00,720
 And the folks at the tippy top of their fields put in a round

76
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:00,780
 .

77
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,500
 10,000 hours of practice.

78
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,300
 Now, we're talking about the game of telephone a little bit earlier.

79
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:09,080
 Here's what happened.

80
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:11,880
 An author by the name of Malcolm Gladwell

81
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:16,380
 wrote a book in 2007 called Outliers, the Story of Success.

82
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:20,300
 And the centerpiece of that book was the 10,000-hour rule.

83
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:24,520
 Practice a lot, practice well, and you will do extremely well.

84
00:05:24,620 --> 00:05:25,660
 You'll reach the top of your field.

85
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:31,140
 So, the message, what Dr. Erickson was actually saying is,

86
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:36,400
 it takes 10,000 hours to get to the top of an ultra-competitive field

87
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,040
 in a very narrow subject.

88
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:39,940
 That's what that means.

89
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:41,820
 But here's what happened.

90
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:45,140
 Ever since Outliers came out, immediately came out,

91
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,080
 reached the top of the bestseller list, stayed there for three solid months,

92
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:52,300
 all of a sudden, the 10,000-hour rule was everywhere.

93
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:58,180
 And a society-wide game of telephone started to be played.

94
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:03,520
 So this message, it takes 10,000 hours to reach the top of an ultra-competitive field,

95
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:08,540
 became, it takes 10,000 hours to become an expert at something.

96
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:14,240
 Which became, it takes 10,000 hours to become good at something.

97
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:19,440
 Which became, it takes 10,000 hours to learn something.

98
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:23,760
 But that last statement, it takes 10,000 hours to learn something,

99
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:25,040
 it's not true.

100
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:26,700
 It's not true.

101
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,620
 So, what the research actually says,

102
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:34,840
 I spent a lot of time here at the CSU library,

103
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:37,880
 in the cognitive psychology stacks, because I'm a geek.

104
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:42,920
 And, when you actually look at the studies of skill acquisition,

105
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:46,320
 you see, over and over and over, a graph like this.

106
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:49,880
 Now, researchers, whether they're studying a motor skill,

107
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,620
 something you do physically, or a mental skill,

108
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,600
 they like to study things that they can time.

109
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:57,440
 Because you can quantify that, right?

110
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:01,400
 So, they'll give research participants a little task,

111
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:03,120
 something that requires physical arrangement,

112
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:07,420
 or something that requires learning a little mental trick.

113
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:11,600
 And they'll time how long a participant takes to complete the skill.

114
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:13,540
 And here's what this graph says.

115
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:17,320
 When you start, so when researchers gave participants a task,

116
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,340
 it took them a really long time,

117
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:20,680
 because it was new and they were horrible.

118
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:25,560
 With a little bit of practice, they get better and better and better,

119
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:29,600
 and that early part of practice is really, really efficient.

120
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:34,060
 People get good at things with just a little bit of practice.

121
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:39,720
 Now, what's interesting to note is that if, you know, we don't really,

122
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:42,000
 for skills that we want to learn for ourselves,

123
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:44,180
 we don't care so much about time, right?

124
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:47,680
 We just care about how good we are, whatever good happens to mean.

125
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:52,260
 So, if we relabel performance time to how good you are,

126
00:07:53,720 --> 00:07:58,080
 the graph flips, and you get this famous and widely known,

127
00:07:58,280 --> 00:07:59,360
 this is the learning curve.

128
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:02,480
 And the story of the learning curve is when you start,

129
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:05,860
 you're grossly incompetent and you know it, right?

130
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:10,620
 With a little bit of practice, you get really good really quick.

131
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:14,180
 So, that early level of improvement is really fast.

132
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:16,880
 And then, at a certain point, you reach a plateau,

133
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:21,020
 and the subsequent gains become much harder to get.

134
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:22,680
 They take more time to get.

135
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:27,240
 Now, my question is, I want that, right?

136
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:30,620
 How long does it take from starting something

137
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:33,160
 and being grossly incompetent and knowing it

138
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:37,520
 to being reasonably good in, hopefully,

139
00:08:37,940 --> 00:08:40,080
 as short a period of time as possible?

140
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:43,080
 So, how long does that take?

141
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:44,820
 Here's what my research says.

142
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:47,080
 20 hours.

143
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:48,980
 That's it.

144
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:54,260
 You can go from knowing nothing about any skill that you can think of.

145
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:55,480
 Want to learn a language?

146
00:08:55,960 --> 00:08:57,260
 Want to learn how to draw?

147
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:00,300
 Want to learn how to juggle flaming chainsaws?

148
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:07,400
 If you put 20 hours of focused, deliberate practice into that thing,

149
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:11,920
 you will be astounded, astounded at how good you are.

150
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:14,440
 20 hours is doable.

151
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:17,580
 That's about 45 minutes a day for about a month,

152
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:20,680
 even skipping a couple days here and there.

153
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:22,860
 20 hours isn't that hard to accumulate.

154
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:26,740
 Now, there's a method to doing this,

155
00:09:26,820 --> 00:09:30,140
 because it's not like you can just start fiddling around for about 20 hours

156
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:31,660
 and expect these massive improvements.

157
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:34,220
 There's a way to practice intelligently.

158
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:36,280
 There's a way to practice efficiently

159
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:39,400
 that will make sure that you invest those 20 hours

160
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:41,980
 in the most effective way that you possibly can.

161
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:43,220
 And here's the method.

162
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:44,080
 It applies to anything.

163
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:50,060
 The first is to deconstruct the skill.

164
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:54,460
 Decide exactly what you want to be able to do when you're done,

165
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:58,060
 and then look into the skill and break it down

166
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:00,280
 into smaller and smaller pieces.

167
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:01,900
 Most of the things...

168
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,500
 that we think of as skills are actually big bundles of skills

169
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:07,640
 that require all sorts of different things.

170
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:09,860
 The more you can break apart the skill,

171
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:13,060
 the more you're able to decide,

172
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:17,580
 what are the parts of the skill that will actually help me get to what I want?

173
00:10:18,340 --> 00:10:20,040
 And then you can practice those first.

174
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:22,860
 And if you practice the most important things first,

175
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:24,980
 you'll be able to improve your performance

176
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:26,860
 in the least amount of time possible.

177
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:30,460
 The second is learn enough to self-correct.

178
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:35,480
 So get three to five resources about what it is you're trying to learn.

179
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:39,520
 Could be books, could be DVDs, could be courses, could be anything.

180
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:45,940
 But don't use those as a way to procrastinate on practice.

181
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:47,300
 I know I do this, right?

182
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:49,420
 Get like 20 books about the topics like,

183
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:52,800
 I'm going to start learning how to program a computer

184
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:54,800
 when I complete these 20 books.

185
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:56,620
 No, that's procrastination.

186
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:59,860
 What you want to do is learn just enough

187
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:07,220
 that you can actually practice and self-correct or self-edit as you practice.

188
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:10,440
 So the learning becomes a way of getting better

189
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:13,060
 at noticing when you're making a mistake,

190
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:15,200
 and then doing something a little different.

191
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:20,240
 The third is to remove barriers to practice.

192
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:23,200
 Distractions, television, internet,

193
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:29,500
 all of these things that get in the way of you actually sitting down and doing the work.

194
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:33,280
 And the more you're able to use just a little bit of willpower

195
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:36,540
 to remove the distractions that are keeping you from practicing,

196
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:42,000
 the more likely you are to actually sit down and practice, right?

197
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,380
 And the fourth is to practice for at least 20 hours.

198
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:51,720
 Now, most skills have what I call a frustration barrier.

199
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:54,340
 You know, the grossly incompetent knowing it part?

200
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:57,320
 That's really, really frustrating.

201
00:11:57,460 --> 00:11:58,460
 We don't like to feel stupid.

202
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:03,580
 And feeling stupid is a barrier to us actually sitting down and doing the work.

203
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:08,760
 So by pre-committing to practicing whatever it is that you want to do

204
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:10,360
 for at least 20 hours,

205
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:15,320
 you will be able to overcome that initial frustration barrier

206
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:19,360
 and stick with the practice long enough to actually reap the rewards.

207
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:21,940
 All right? That's it. It's not rocket science.

208
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:27,160
 Four very simple steps that you can use to learn anything.

209
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:31,640
 Now, this is easy to talk about in theory,

210
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:34,300
 but it's more fun to talk about in practice.

211
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:38,400
 So one of the things that I've wanted to learn how to do for a long time

212
00:12:39,260 --> 00:12:40,480
 is play the ukulele.

213
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:44,960
 Has anybody seen Jake Shimabukuro's TED Talk

214
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:47,340
 where he plays the ukulele and makes it sound like,

215
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:49,040
 he's like an ukulele god.

216
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:50,340
 It's amazing.

217
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:52,260
 It's like, I saw that.

218
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:54,100
 It's like, that is so cool.

219
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:55,080
 It's such a neat instrument.

220
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:57,680
 I would really like to learn how to play.

221
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:01,100
 And so I decided that to test this theory,

222
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:04,460
 I wanted to put 20 hours into practicing the ukulele

223
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:06,000
 and see where we got.

224
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:10,580
 And so the first thing about playing the ukulele

225
00:13:10,580 --> 00:13:13,460
 is in order to practice, you have to have one.

226
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:14,280
 Right?

227
00:13:14,280 --> 00:13:16,660
 So I got an ukulele.

228
00:13:17,020 --> 00:13:19,120
 And my lovely assistant.

229
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:22,360
 Thank you, sir.

230
00:13:22,780 --> 00:13:25,980
 I think I need the cord here.

231
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:29,960
 It's not just an ukulele.

232
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:31,500
 It's an electric ukulele.

233
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:33,120
 Yeah.

234
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:41,980
 So the first couple hours are just like the first couple hours of anything.

235
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:45,560
 You have to get the tools that you're using to practice.

236
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:47,200
 You have to make sure that they're available.

237
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:49,760
 My ukulele didn't come with strings attached.

238
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:51,460
 I had to figure out how to put those on.

239
00:13:51,660 --> 00:13:52,540
 Like, that's kind of important.

240
00:13:52,760 --> 00:13:52,840
 Right?

241
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:55,540
 And learning how to tune and learning how to make sure

242
00:13:55,540 --> 00:13:57,180
 that all of the things that need to be done

243
00:13:57,180 --> 00:13:59,800
 in order to start practicing get done.

244
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:00,260
 Right?

245
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:07,160
 Now, one of the things when I was ready to actually start practicing

246
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:11,080
 was I looked in online databases and songbooks

247
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:13,300
 for how to play songs.

248
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:14,840
 And they say, okay, ukuleles,

249
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:16,320
 you can play more than one string at a time,

